    "https://jobs.okstate.edu/jobs/assistant-associate-professor-210218-tulsa-oklahoma-united-states": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor 210218",
      "description": "Assistant/Associate Professor 210218 - Tulsa, Oklahoma, United States Assistant/Associate Professor 210218 - Tulsa, Oklahoma, United States Kiosk mode Assistant/Associate Professor 210218 Apply Now Assistant/Associate Professor 210218 495171 Tulsa CENTER FOR HEALTH SCIENCES (CHS) Faculty PhD Campus OSU-Center for Health Sciences Contact Name & Email Jamie Childers, Jamie.Childers@okstate.edu Work Schedule Typically Monday - Friday, 8 hour shifts Appointment Length Regular Continuous/Until Further Notice Hiring Range $ Salary Priority Application Date While applications will be accepted until a successful candidate has been hired, interested parties are encouraged to submit their materials by to ensure full consideration. Special Instructions to Applicants A cover letter and current CV are required About this Position Tenure-track, Full-Time Faculty Position at OSU-CHS, Tulsa, OK. This position offers an exciting opportunity to join an integrated, innovative, learner-centered academic program. Duties of an assistant professor include research, teaching (medical and graduate courses), and service/outreach. Competitive start-up funding is available. Reporting Responsibility: Reports directly to the Chair of the Department of Pharmacology and Physiology for Reappointment, Promotion, and Tenure (RPT). Required Qualifications Ph.D./Ed.D. Ph.D. in neuroscience, pharmacology or a related discipline is required Postdoctoral experience in the relevant discipline is also required Skills, Proficiencies, and/or Knowledge: Real-world therapeutic impact, whether through pre-clinical models and pharmacology, studies of human tissues and biomarkers, or direct investigation in human participants. The successful candidate will be well suited to build their program utilizing the new OSU-CHS vivarium, OSU Biomedical Imaging Center (e.g., fMRI, TMS, fNIRS, EEG), and future OSU Bioincubator and Clinical Research Hub, leveraging CHS’ infrastructure to accelerate the translation of basic findings into candidate interventions. Scientists whose work intersects with OSU-CHS strategic priorities in childhood adversity, addiction and motivated behaviors, and the development of novel therapeutics, and who are eager to grow collaborations across preclinical and clinical domains. Candidates at all career stages are encouraged to apply, and we especially welcome those whose research vision emphasizes interdisciplinary partnership and a clear path toward improving human health. Translational neuroscience, neuropharmacology, neural basis of substance abuse, neuroinflammation, or neurotoxicology. Good communication and interpersonal skills are preferred to facilitate interactions with faculty, students, and administration. Ability to perform in a collegial and professional manner. Preferred Qualifications History of securing extramural funding preferred. All positions are subject to the availability of funding, and any offers are contingent upon the successful completion of a background check. Federal law requires employers to provide reasonable accommodation to qualified individuals with disabilities. Please tell us if you require a reasonable accommodation in the application process. Examples of reasonable accommodation include making a change to the application process, providing documents in an alternate format, using a sign language interpreter, or using specialized equipment. To request an accommodation, please email eeo@okstate.edu or call 405-744-5449. Oklahoma State University, as an equal opportunity employer, complies with all applicable federal and state laws regarding non-discrimination. Oklahoma State University is committed to a policy of equal opportunity for all individuals and does not discriminate based on race, religion, age, sex, color, national origin, marital status, disability, or veteran status with regard to employment, educational programs and activities, and/or admissions. For more information, visit eeo.okstate.e